\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Coordinate covariance and its Gaussian density}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Let #{Q} be a [positive precision parameter](fdip-0004) on a
finite real inner-product space #{(E,h)}, and use its
inverse-precision covariance formula in \ref{fdip-000B}.
For a chosen nonorthonormal basis #{b_i}, the coordinate functional is
#{h(b^i,\cdot)}. The dual basis satisfies #{h(b^i,b_j)=\delta_{ij}}.
Its covariance array
therefore has entries #{h(b^i,Q^{-1}b^j)}; it is not the unqualified
array of the inverse operator in that basis. The counting basis is
orthonormal, giving the coordinate formula in \ref{fdip-000B}.
The alternating-cycle example \ref{fdip-0037} evaluates it explicitly.}
\p{To compare with formal Gaussian providers, put #{n=\dim E}, choose
an orthonormal chart #{C:E\to\mathbb R^n}, and write #{y=Cx}.
Let #{S=CQ^{-1}C^{-1}} be the array in this chart. The positive inverse
is positive definite, #{S^{-1}=CQC^{-1}}, and
#{\det S=(\det Q)^{-1}}. Consequently the provider density is
##{\begin{aligned}
 &(2\pi)^{-n/2}(\det S)^{-1/2}
       e^{-y^TS^{-1}y/2}\\
 &\qquad=Z_Q^{-1}e^{-h(x,Qx)/2}.
\end{aligned}}
The [canonical-volume characterization](fdip-002M), using
\ref{fdip-002L} for isometries between counting coordinate spaces,
makes #{C} volume preserving. The Euclidean-space coordinate wrapper
also preserves volume, as in
\leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr} and
\leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_toLp}. After this wrapper, the chart
pushforward of #{\mu_Q} is #{\mathrm{multivariateGaussian}(0,S)} by
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.multivariateGaussian_eq_withDensity}.
Mathlib's
\leanref{ProbabilityTheory.covariance_eval_multivariateGaussian}
then identifies its coordinate covariance with #{S}; its positive
semidefinite hypothesis follows from positive definiteness. The
parameter is the covariance array #{S} of #{Q^{-1}}, not the precision
array #{CQC^{-1}}. This density,
determinant and volume identification is needed in addition to the
covariance declaration; the integral proof in \ref{fdip-000B} is
independent of it.}

\p{The density comparison uses
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505, and Theorem 8.4, p. 385}{friedli2017statistical},
with zero mean and covariance #{S} in the specified orthonormal
chart. The nonorthonormal coordinate entries follow by applying
\ref{fdip-000B} to the displayed dual-basis functionals.}
